// Lesson 4.5 — The Jump: Getting the Player Off the Ground
// REWRITTEN. This slot used to hold the enemy state-machine capstone. The class
// has moved into the cave, the enemy work is on hold, and this hour is now the
// first cave lesson: the player gets gravity in one scene and one line that
// pushes it off the ground.
//
// Scaffolding: FULL WALKTHROUGH (level 1). A jump has no precedent anywhere in
// the course — the student has moved a transform (1.4), set a velocity (2.2) and
// read a key (1.4), but has never changed a body's momentum in one instant. So
// every line is given and explained, and the lesson is about WHERE each line
// goes and WHY the push is an impulse rather than a force.
//
// STRUCTURE (editor/code split):
//   - "In the Editor" is the observation half: play the cave, watch the body
//     refuse to fall, turn Gravity Scale up live, watch Stop throw it away,
//     break the hub with the same number, and find the leftover `Is Platformer`
//     override the scene has been carrying.
//   - "In the Code" is the file half: one API table (four rows), one
//     architectural specification, one Socratic, then the single level-1
//     walkthrough.
//   - "Build Lab: Tune It" is the feel pass, because a jump is a number before
//     it is a feature.
//
// GROUND TRUTH (read from the project by earlier lessons and reused here; do
// not "improve" these numbers without opening the project):
//   - Unity 6000.6.0f1, legacy Input class: Input.GetKeyDown is correct.
//     Rigidbody2D.linearVelocity, not the old `velocity`.
//   - EditorBuildSettings orders the scenes Start(0), Overworld(1),
//     Platformer(2). The cave is Platformer; the cave mouth in the Overworld
//     loads it through the project's own SceneSwitch.cs.
//   - Physics2DSettings ships m_Gravity {x: 0, y: -9.81}; Gravity Scale is a
//     multiplier on it, per body.
//   - Player.prefab READ AS A PREFAB: root Transform + PlayerController; the
//     SpriteRenderer and Animator live on a child called `Sprite`. There is NO
//     Rigidbody2D and NO Collider2D on the prefab — those are the student's
//     work from 2.1, which is also where Gravity Scale was set to 0 for a
//     top-down world.
//   - THE FINDING: the Platformer scene holds the Player as a PREFAB INSTANCE
//     whose modification list carries `propertyPath: isPlatformer / value: 1`
//     targeting PlayerController. The field does not exist in the stripped
//     starter, so today it does nothing — a leftover from the project's own
//     build. Unity keys those overrides by field NAME, so declaring a
//     serialized field with exactly that name picks the tick back up. The
//     walkthrough says so and tells the student to check the box rather than
//     trust it.
//   - The cave's Collision tilemap is a Static body whose Inspector reads
//     Gravity Scale 1, greyed out. Do not call that "Gravity Scale 0".
//
// PROTECTED BLOCKS — ids are POSITIONAL and persisted. This page keeps the
// committed shape exactly: one decompose block with THREE turns (4.5:dc1:q1..q3)
// and one Socratic with THREE questions (4.5:sc1:q1..q3). Every question was
// reworded in place; none was inserted, deleted, moved or reordered, so the
// saved answers under those ids still belong to the prompts they were typed
// into. `what: ''` appears only on blank spacer lines, never on a line carrying
// a `why` or `hint`.
//
// CULLED FOR REPETITION: no trap callout repeats the API table's Gravity Scale
// row; the walkthrough's step 2 explains why the gravity write is code rather
// than an Inspector number, so no paragraph above it says that again. The
// lesson names the enemy nowhere: it is on hold.
import Lesson from '../../layouts/LessonLayout.astro';
import { renderWalkthrough, renderAskBanner } from '../../lib/walkthrough';
import { renderDecompose } from '../../lib/decompose';

// The two recurring questions, applied to the jump. Level 1, so every part is
// given with its `does` and its `doc`. Five parts: three the student has met
// before wearing another subject, and two that are genuinely new.
const DECOMPOSE = {
  big:
    'The player should leap off the ground in the cave, and right now nothing in this project ' +
