  parts: [
    {
      name: 'Give the Player a body that falls in this scene',
      does: 'Body Type **Dynamic** and Gravity Scale **above zero** - it takes both. Gravity Scale multiplies the project gravity Physics 2D holds at `-9.81` on Y. Your body has been Dynamic at Gravity Scale 0 since 2.1, because a top-down character who obeys gravity walks off the edge of the world.',
      doc: 'the `rb.gravityScale` row of the quick reference.',
      href: '../docs/reference/unity-csharp-quickref.html',
    },
    {
      name: 'Tell the script which world it woke up in',
      does: 'The same Player prefab is played in both worlds, so "am I in the cave?" is a fact about the *scene*: one serialized `bool`, ticked on the cave copy and left alone on the hub copy - the same field shape you have used since 1.3, pointed at the world instead of at the player.',
      doc: 'the `[SerializeField] private` row in the quick reference, and the field rules from 1.3.',
      href: '1.3.html',
    },
    {
      name: 'Turn gravity on once, when the scene loads',
      does: 'A gated write in `Start()`: if the flag is on, hand the body a gravity scale. Once, before the first frame - in `Update()` the same number is written sixty times a second for a value that never changes.',
      doc: 'the `void Start()` row of the quick reference.',
      href: '../docs/reference/unity-csharp-quickref.html',
    },
    {
      name: 'Notice one press of the jump key',
      does: '`Input.GetKeyDown(KeyCode.Space)` inside the `Update()` you have been adding to since 1.4. It is true on the single frame the key goes down; `Input.GetKey(KeyCode.Space)` is true on every frame it is held - a jump is a moment, not a state.',
      doc: 'the `Input.GetKeyDown(KeyCode.Space)` row of the quick reference, and the input functions from 1.4.',
      href: '1.4.html',
    },
    {
      name: 'Hand the body one upward flick, and let gravity spend it',
      does: '`AddForce(Vector2.up * jumpForce, ForceMode2D.Impulse)`: one instant change in momentum. The height is the impulse divided by mass, the rise is paid for by the gravity from part 1, and the apex is the frame the vertical velocity reaches zero.',
      doc: 'the `rb.AddForce(... ForceMode2D.Impulse)` row of the quick reference.',
      href: '../docs/reference/unity-csharp-quickref.html',
    },
  ],
  why:
    'Two of those five parts are new to this course, and they are the two that make a jump: a ' +
    'number that changes momentum in one instant, and a world that spends it. The other three ' +
    'are work you have already done - a Dynamic body is 2.1, a serialized field is 1.3, a key ' +
    'check is 1.4 - which is why this lesson can hand you every line and still be worth the ' +
    'hour. The new part is one call; the work is choosing where it goes.',
  sizeTest:
    'The test: could you look this part up on its own, right now, in one search? "Make the ' +
    'player jump" cannot be looked up - there is no such API. "Push a rigidbody upward once" ' +
    'can, and the answer is one line long.',
  turns: [
    'Parts two and three are the same trick as every Inspector checkbox since 1.3: a field holds a fact, and code reads it. What fact about the *scene* - not about the player - does the flag hold, and where would you look to prove it is ticked?',
    'Nothing in the jump code measures time, and yet the arc has a height. Where does the height come from, and which part would you change first if the jump felt wrong?',
    'The flick is spent in one frame, but the rise takes about half a second. Which part of the five is doing the work in the frames where no key is pressed?',
  ],
};

// The single walkthrough. Level 1: every line is given, because every line is
// either new or a placement decision the lesson is actually teaching.
const WALK = {
  ask: 'how do I make the player jump in the cave?',
  file: 'Assets/Scripts/PlayerController.cs',
  label: 'In the Code',
  intro:
    'There is no `Jump()` in Unity, and nobody is going to give you one. What is left after the ' +
    'editor half is two fields, one gated gravity line, one key check, one push and a way to see ' +
    'that it worked. The push is where every mistake in this lesson happens.',
  level: 1,
  steps: [
    {
      title: 'Declare how hard the flick is, and which world you are in',
      lead: 'Two fields in the class body beside `moveSpeed`, for the same reason `moveSpeed` is there: they are numbers and facts a person tunes, not values you bury inside a condition.',
      lines: [
        {
          code: '    [SerializeField] private float jumpForce = 7f;',
          what: '**How hard the flick is.** Named `jumpForce` because that is what the call takes - a force - so no reader mistakes it for the speed the character leaves the ground at.',
          why: '**A starting guess, not a value.** The number that makes a jump feel right is found in the tuning lab at the bottom of this page. And a force is divided by mass before it becomes motion, so the height `7f` buys depends on the mass the Inspector shows.',
        },
        { code: '', what: '' },
        {
          code: '    [SerializeField] private bool isPlatformer = false;',
          what: '**Which world this Player woke up in.** `false` is the default, so a Player in any scene nobody has ticked keeps the top-down behaviour it has had since 2.1.',
          why: '**The name is not free, and you can check why.** Click the cave\'s Player and look at `PlayerController`: the scene has carried an override called `Is Platformer` since before this course existed - a leftover from the version of this script that shipped with the project. Unity stores overrides by field **name**, so this spelling picks the tick back up where `inCave` would lose it. Check the box either way.',
        },
      ],
    },
    {
      title: 'Turn gravity on once, in a method that runs once',
      lead: 'The body lookup you already do in `Start()` is unchanged. What is new is one line underneath it, wrapped in the flag.',
      lines: [
        {
          code: '        body = GetComponent<Rigidbody2D>();',
          what: 'The reference you already cache here - the body about to be given a gravity scale. `GetComponent<Rigidbody2D>()` finds the component on the same GameObject as this script.',
          why: '**Leave it exactly where it is.** A lookup in `Start()` happens once; the same lookup inside `Update()` would run every frame for a reference that never changes. That is the rule you have followed since you first cached anything.',
        },
        { code: '', what: '' },
        {
          code: '        if (isPlatformer)',
          what: 'The gate. Read it as "if this Player is standing in the cave". It is the only thing separating the two worlds.',
          why: '**Read once, in a method that runs once.** `Start()` is the once-only setup method from 1.3, and a fact about the scene belongs there.',
        },
        { code: '        {', what: '' },
        {
          code: '            body.gravityScale = 1f;',
          what: '**The whole of turning gravity on.** `body.gravityScale` is the Rigidbody2D property behind the Inspector field you have been reading all year, and `1f` means "the project gravity, undiluted".',
          why: '**Why this is code and not a number in the Inspector.** You could set the cave copy to 1 by hand and write nothing - but that number is invisible to a reader of your script, and it has to be repeated on every new Player anyone places.',
        },
        { code: '        }', what: '' },
      ],
    },
    {
      title: 'Notice the press, and spend it in one line',
      lead: 'Four lines at the bottom of the `Update()` you have been adding to since 1.4 - after your movement and your animation lines, where the frame already knows what the player is doing.',
      lines: [
        {
          code: '        if (Input.GetKeyDown(KeyCode.Space))',
          what: '`Input` is the class from 1.4, `KeyCode.Space` names the key, and `Input.GetKeyDown(KeyCode.Space)` is true on the **single frame** the key goes down.',
          why: '**The wrong key check fails in the loudest way there is.** Without `Down`, the check is true on every frame the key is held, so half a second of holding Space applies thirty pushes and the character leaves the top of the level. The jump looks broken; the condition is. And the check belongs in `Update()`: `FixedUpdate()` is the physics clock, which can run several times in one frame or skip frames, so a one-frame press can be counted twice or missed.',
        },
        { code: '        {', what: '' },
        {
          code: '            body.AddForce(Vector2.up * jumpForce, ForceMode2D.Impulse);',
          what: '**The flick.** `Vector2.up` is `(0, 1)` - straight up - scaled by the field from step 1. Read the call as a sentence: add a force, upward, of this size, as an impulse.',
          why: '**The second argument is the whole lesson, and leaving it out is the classic bug.** With `ForceMode2D.Impulse` this is one hit, spent at the next physics step. Without it the default is a force that keeps applying while the calls keep coming - and since this line sits inside a check true on exactly one frame, you get a dribble so small the character twitches and settles. Nothing errors, nothing warns, and "my jump does nothing" is what you write in the group chat.',
        },
        { code: '        }', what: '' },
      ],
    },
    {
      title: 'Prove the arc in the Console, then take the print out',
      lead: 'There is no UI for vertical velocity, so the Console is your instrument. One temporary print, at the bottom of `Update()` where every frame runs.',
      lines: [
        {
          code: '        Debug.Log("v.y = " + body.linearVelocity.y);',
          what: 'At the **bottom of `Update()`**, outside the key block, with no condition on it. `body.linearVelocity` is how fast the body is travelling, read instead of written, and `.y` is the half a jump cares about.',
          why: '**That number is the parabola.** It jumps to a peak when you press Space, shrinks as gravity takes it back, crosses zero at the top of the arc, and grows negative all the way down. The apex is not a place in your code - it is the frame this number passes through nothing.',
        },
        {
          code: '// Delete the print before you move on',
          what: 'An instrument, not a feature. A print left here fills the Console with a thousand lines a minute and buries the next real error under them.',
        },
      ],
    },
  ],
  finalFile: `using UnityEngine;

// PlayerController.cs - the jump, complete.
// Everything from 1.4 to 4.4 (input, movement, animation, coins, health)
// stays exactly as you wrote it. The flag, the gravity line, the key check
// and the push are the whole of what is new.
public class PlayerController : MonoBehaviour
{
    [SerializeField] private float moveSpeed = 5f;      // your number
    [SerializeField] private float jumpForce = 7f;      // how hard the flick is
    [SerializeField] private bool isPlatformer = false; // which world did I wake up in?

    private Rigidbody2D body;

    void Start()
    {
        // Looked up once, never inside Update() - the rule from 2.1.
        body = GetComponent<Rigidbody2D>();

        // Decided once, for both worlds. The hub's 0 is written here rather
        // than inherited from the prefab, so the decision is visible to
        // anyone reading this file instead of only to the Inspector.
        body.gravityScale = isPlatformer ? 1f : 0f;
    }

    void Update()
    {
        // ... input, movement and animation, from 1.4 to 4.4 ...

        if (Input.GetKeyDown(KeyCode.Space))
        {
            Jump();
        }
    }

    // The whole jump. One impulse, and gravity spends it over the next half
    // second - which is why there is nothing here about coming back down.
    private void Jump()
    {
        body.AddForce(Vector2.up * jumpForce, ForceMode2D.Impulse);
    }
}`,
  finalNote:
    'Compare against your own file rather than copying it. Two differences are worth naming, and neither is a mistake. First the shape: the walkthrough wrote the push inline inside the key check, and this file gives it a method called `Jump()` - the same line, with a name you can point at when a ground check asks what a jump is allowed to do. Second the gravity line: this file decides the number for both worlds with a ternary where the walkthrough only wrote it for one. Your key, your number and the names of your own methods are yours.',
};

const body = `<h1>4.5 — The Jump: Getting the Player Off the Ground</h1>

<p class="lesson-lead">
    The cave is the first place in this game where <em>down</em> means something. Walk off the
    edge of the floor and you do not fall — you keep walking, level. Today you turn gravity back
    on for one scene, and use the same body to throw the character into the air.
</p>

${renderAskBanner('in unity how to apply a jump force, how to get the name of the current scene, how to adjust the gravity of a rigidbody 2d, how to get a key press', 1)}

${renderDecompose('4.5', DECOMPOSE, 1)}

<div class="activity discovery">
    <div class="activity-header">
        <span class="activity-label">In the Editor</span>
        <span class="activity-time">~20 min</span>
    </div>

    <ol>
        <li>
            <strong>Project window →</strong> open <code>Assets/_Scenes/Platformer.unity</code>
            — the cave — then <strong>Game view →</strong> press Play and walk off the edge of
            the floor. Nothing falls, and that is not a bug: the Rigidbody2D you gave the Player
            in <a href="2.1.html">2.1</a> has <strong>Gravity Scale 0</strong>, because a
            top-down character who obeys gravity falls through the world forever. You were right
            then. The cave is the first place where you are wrong.
        </li>
        <li>
            <strong>Hierarchy →</strong> select the <strong>Player</strong>, then
            <strong>Inspector → Rigidbody 2D → Gravity Scale</strong>. Set it to <code>1</code>
            <em>while the game is running</em> and watch the character drop. Then try
            <code>0.2</code> and describe the difference out loud.
        </li>
        <li>
            <strong>Game view →</strong> stop Play, then <strong>Inspector → Gravity Scale</strong>:
            it is back to <code>0</code>. Inspector edits made during Play are thrown away on Stop
            (1.2) — which is why the cave's gravity has to end up in code.
        </li>
        <li>
            <strong>Hierarchy →</strong> open the <code>Overworld</code> scene — the top-down hub
            — select its <strong>Player</strong>, and set <strong>Inspector → Gravity Scale</strong>
            to <code>1</code>. <strong>Game view →</strong> press Play: you have just broken the
            hub. Write down what it cost, then set it back to <code>0</code>.
        </li>
        <li>
            <strong>Hierarchy →</strong> back in the cave, select the Player and read
            <strong>Inspector → Player Controller</strong>. There is a leftover override called
            <strong>Is Platformer</strong> that no field in your script matches yet. Leave it —
            step 1 of the walkthrough below is about it.
        </li>
        <li>
            <strong>Once the walkthrough is done — Hierarchy →</strong> the Player standing in the
            <em>cave</em>, <strong>Inspector → Player Controller → Is Platformer</strong>, and tick
            it. Confirm the <code>Overworld</code>'s copy is unticked, then
            <strong>Game view →</strong> press Play in both: the cave falls, the hub does not.
        </li>
    </ol>
</div>

<div class="activity basic-app">
    <div class="activity-header">
        <span class="activity-label">In the Code</span>
        <span class="activity-time">~30 min</span>
    </div>

    <p>Four calls do all of it — the key, the gravity, the push, and the instrument that proves the push worked:</p>

    <table>
        <tr><th>API</th><th>What it is</th><th>What you get</th><th>What it costs</th></tr>
        <tr>
            <td><code>Input.GetKeyDown(KeyCode.Space)</code></td>
            <td>A key check that is true for exactly one frame.</td>
            <td>A press you can act on once.</td>
            <td>Nothing — unless you reach for <code>Input.GetKey(KeyCode.Space)</code> instead. That one is true every frame the key is held, and a jump built on it is a jetpack.</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td><code>rb.gravityScale</code></td>
            <td>A multiplier on the project gravity, set per body.</td>
            <td>Down, at whatever strength you choose.</td>
            <td>It means nothing on a Kinematic body. A body that ignores gravity still accepts a push, and then rises forever.</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td><code>rb.AddForce(Vector2.up * jumpForce, ForceMode2D.Impulse)</code></td>
            <td>One instant change in momentum.</td>
            <td>A parabola that gravity finishes for you.</td>
            <td>Mass divides into it, so retuning mass retunes every jump. Drop the force mode and the push becomes a dribble instead of a jump.</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td><code>rb.linearVelocity</code></td>
            <td>How fast the body is travelling, in units per second.</td>
            <td>A number you can read to watch the arc.</td>
            <td>Reading it is free. Writing it erases whatever vertical motion was already there — a platform lift, a knockback, a bounce.</td>
        </tr>
    </table>

    <div class="callout">
        <strong>Architectural specification: the gravity and the flick.</strong>
        <ul>
            <li>Where does the cave's gravity get turned on — a field default, <code>Start()</code>, or every frame? Say what breaks at the other two.</li>
            <li>Which key did you choose, and is the check <code>Input.GetKeyDown(KeyCode.Space)</code> or <code>Input.GetKey(KeyCode.Space)</code>? Say what holding the key for two seconds does under each.</li>
            <li>Is your jump force a serialized field or a number typed into the condition? Does its name say <em>force</em> or <em>speed</em> — and what does the name make the next reader believe?</li>
            <li>How do you know the jump worked? Name what you would print, and what it proves.</li>
        </ul>
    </div>

    <div class="socratic">
        <h3>Socratic: Gravity Is a Number You Own</h3>
        <ol>
            <li>Real gravity is a constant of the universe. Unity ships it as an <em>Inspector field</em> you can set per object. Why would an engine make a law of physics editable?</li>
            <li>Same Player prefab, same script, two worlds: the hub needs Gravity Scale 0 and the cave needs it above 0. Where should that difference live — the prefab, the scene, or the script — and what does each choice cost the next person who places a Player?</li>
            <li>A Kinematic body ignores gravity, forces and mass entirely. Set Body Type to Kinematic in the cave, and what would your jump code have to do instead to look like a jump?</li>
        </ol>
    </div>
</div>

${renderWalkthrough(WALK)}

<div class="activity basic-app">
    <div class="activity-header">
        <span class="activity-label">Build Lab: Tune It</span>
        <span class="activity-time">~15 min</span>
    </div>

    <p>
        Change one number at a time and write one sentence of feel for each, in a file you keep.
        Three knobs, and the third one is the interesting one.
    </p>

    <table>
        <tr><th>Knob</th><th>Try</th></tr>
        <tr><td><code>jumpForce</code> — Inspector → Player Controller</td><td>half, then double</td></tr>
        <tr><td><code>Gravity Scale</code> — Inspector → Rigidbody 2D</td><td>0.2, then 1, then 4</td></tr>
        <tr><td><code>Mass</code> — Inspector → Rigidbody 2D</td><td>1, then 5 — watch the jump <em>and</em> the fall</td></tr>
    </table>

    <p>
        Then walk back through the cave mouth into the hub and jump there. It should do nothing —
        and if it does something, read Gravity Scale on the Overworld's Player before you read
        your code.
    </p>
</div>

<div class="checklist">
    <h3>Checkpoint — before moving on, you must be able to:</h3>
    <ul>
        <li>Break "make the player jump" into its parts before you write any code, and name the earlier lesson you lifted each one from</li>
        <li>Say why the cave needs gravity on and the hub needs it off, and where that difference is decided</li>
        <li>Write a key check that fires on one frame, and say what the held-key version does instead</li>
        <li>Explain what <code>ForceMode2D.Impulse</code> changes about the push, and what happens without it</li>
        <li>Read <code>body.linearVelocity.y</code> in the Console and point at the apex without a UI</li>
        <li>Commit and push</li>
    </ul>
</div>

<div class="resources">
    <h3>Reference Docs</h3>
    <p>Need a deeper explanation? These pages cover the same concepts:</p>
    <ul>
        <li><a href="../docs/reference/unity-csharp-quickref.html">Unity + C# Quick Reference</a> — <code>gravityScale</code>, the key check and the impulse call</li>
        <li><a href="../docs/unit-02/index.html">Unit 2: Stats &amp; Pickups</a> — the Rigidbody2D and collision work this lesson stands on</li>
        <li><a href="../docs/unit-06/index.html">Unit 6: Jumping</a> — where the jump goes next: ground checks, feel and a second jump</li>
    </ul>
</div>`;
